id: design.producer-consumer
kind: design
title: Producer / consumer
aliases: producer consumer, queue decoupling, decoupling
question: how do you decouple a fast producer from a slow consumer?
tags: architecture, concurrency
questions:
  - how do you decouple a fast producer from a slow consumer?
invariant: A queue between the two sides decouples their rates; the queue depth is then a first-class observable.
answer_30s: Put a bounded queue between producer and consumer so each side runs at its own pace, and make queue depth the signal for scaling and alerting.
answer_2m: |
  What decoupling buys: the producer's latency stops depending on the consumer's
  latency, producers and consumers scale independently, and a consumer restart
  does not lose work (if the queue is durable). What it costs: a second thing to
  operate, ordering is now per-partition rather than global, and the system has
  two failure modes instead of one. The interview-worthy distinction is
  in-process versus external queue: a channel is cheap and vanishes on restart
  (fine for derived work), a broker survives and needs idempotent consumers,
  offsets and replay semantics. Deciding which is required is the actual design
  question.
when_to_use:
  - work with bursty arrival and slow processing; an existing queue already in the stack
avoid_when:
  - the producer needs the result synchronously (that is a call, not a queue)
failure_modes:
  - unbounded queue so the consumer's slowness is hidden until memory runs out
  - non-idempotent consumers on a queue that redelivers (at-least-once)
  - ordering assumed globally when the broker only guarantees it per partition
related:
  - design.bounded-queue
  - design.worker-pool
  - design.idempotency
  - rust.tokio-mpsc
  - recipe.async-job-processing
sources:
  - https://aws.amazon.com/builders-library/avoiding-insurmountable-queue-backlogs/
